    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "thanks for the info. i think it might actually be fat bloom rather than sugar bloom though, because the texture feels a bit different. can you go into more detail about the cocoa butter crystals? specifically why certain crystals form over others during the tempering stage and how i should be adjusting my temperature curve to make sure i only get the stable ones?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Fat bloom happens when cocoa butter migrates to the surface either from temperature fluctuations (melt bloom) or oxidation (sugar bloom). During tempering, you manipulate temperatures to encourage the formation of stable Form V crystals within the cocoa butter structure. The goal of your temperature curve is to first melt all crystals, then cool to nucleate many small, desirable Form V seeds, and finally reheat slightly to allow those stable crystals to grow before cooling again. This specific manipulation locks the cocoa butter into its most stable crystalline state, preventing unwanted migration and cloudiness.",
